# Test: the reconnect backoff forgets its penalty after a stable session
#
# The one question: does the running daemon, on its real clock, double the
# reconnect delay after sessions that end soon after Established, and go back to
# the minimum after a session that stayed Established longer than reconnectMax?
#
# Why a .ci: the unit tests in internal/component/bgp/reactor/
# peer_run_backoff_test.go decide the rule through Peer.run on a fake clock.
# Only the daemon shows that a config-built peer, the 60 s reconnectMax that
# NewPeer sets (DefaultReconnectMax, peer.go), the real peer clock and the error
# a peer's TCP close produces reach that rule.
#
# Spec: spec-single-owner-lifecycle-2-bgp-session (closed; plan/learned/048-session-owns-its-sync.md),
# AC-8 (stable session restarts at reconnectMin), AC-9 (short session doubles),
# user stories 2 and 3. R-8: the stable time is not shortened and has no knob,
# so this test holds a session for over a minute and takes about 85 to 115 s.
#
# What it asserts. The producer is Peer.run (peer_run.go). It logs
# "timing: backoff starting" with delay=<base>, the delay BEFORE fsm.Jitter
# scales it to 0.75..1.0 of the base, so every assertion below reads an exact
# base (5s, 10s, 20s) and never a waited time. Each backoff line is pinned to
# the cycle it follows by two lines of the same goroutine: "timing:
# safeRunOnce returned ... attempt=N" before it and "timing: connection
# attempt starting ... attempt=N+1" after it. So each window holds exactly one
# backoff line, and a later reset cannot satisfy an earlier window.
#
#   cycle 1  Established, ends at once      next base 5s   (reconnectMin)
#   cycle 2  Established, ends at once      next base 10s  (doubled: AC-9)
#   cycle 3  Established for over a minute  next base 5s   (restarted: AC-8)
#                                           the unfixed daemon logs 20s
#   cycle 4  ze dialed again                (closes the cycle 3 window)
#
# How the sessions end. ze-peer closes the TCP connection when a connection's
# expectations are met (no linger), which ze reads as ErrConnectionClosed
# (session_read.go): an ordinary error, not ErrTeardown. ze is the only dialer:
# `accept false` opens no listener, and ze-peer never connects, so no inbound
# connection wakes a wait. No command is sent, so no administrative wake
# either. Each of those three resets the delay on its own and would hide the
# rule.
#
# How cycle 3 is held for over a minute without a sleep. Hold time 90 and
# keepalive 30 are written below (both are the YANG defaults), and ze-peer
# states no smaller hold time, so ze sends a KEEPALIVE every 30 s scaled by the
# RFC 4271 Section 10 jitter to 22.5..30 s (Timers.StartKeepaliveTimer,
# fsm/timer.go). Connection 3 waits for three of them after the End-of-RIB, at
# least 67.5 s after ze entered Established, before ze-peer closes it. The wait
# is the daemon's own timer, and the 7.5 s margin over the 60 s stable time
# holds whatever the jitter draws.
#
# Each End-of-RIB expectation proves the cycle reached Established: RFC 4271
# Section 9 permits an UPDATE only there.
#
# Mutation-verified 2026-11-12 against a daemon built through a GOFLAGS=-overlay
# copy of peer_run.go whose Peer.cycleStayedEstablished returns `false && ...`,
# which is the daemon before the fix (the shared tree was not touched):
#   * the run FAILS in 110.2s. The cycle 1 and cycle 2 windows and the cycle 3
#     elapsed precondition pass (VERIFY steps 3, 4, 5), and the cycle 3 window
#     goes RED (step 6: no delay=5s between attempt=3 and attempt=4). The
#     mutated daemon's own log shows the delay still doubling: `timing: backoff
#     starting ... delay=40s` after attempt 4.
#   * the run stopped at step 6, so the cycle 3 reject=stderr (delay=20s) was
#     not reached in that run. It is kept as the wrong-outcome check, not
#     claimed as observed red.
# The same file passed against the fixed daemon on 2026-11-12 in 98.4s.
stdin=peer:terminator=EOF_PEER
option=tcp_connections:value=4
option=asn:value=65001
# Connection 1: ze's IPv4 End-of-RIB, then ze-peer closes.
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00170200000000
# Connection 2: the same short session.
expect=bgp:conn=2:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00170200000000
# Connection 3: End-of-RIB, then three of ze's periodic KEEPALIVEs, then close.
# ze's handshake KEEPALIVE arrives before the End-of-RIB and is accepted in
# silence (Checker.expectedOrKeepalive), so the three below are periodic ones.
expect=bgp:conn=3:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00170200000000
expect=bgp:conn=3:seq=2:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF001304
expect=bgp:conn=3:seq=3:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF001304
expect=bgp:conn=3:seq=4:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF001304
# Connection 4 exists only if ze dialed after the cycle 3 backoff, so the
# runner cannot stop ze before the cycle 3 backoff line is written.
expect=bgp:conn=4:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00170200000000
EOF_PEER

# ze bgp configuration: one active peer, no listener, the default timers
# written out because the cycle 3 hold is computed from them.
stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
bgp {
	filter {
		import {
			final accept
		}
		export {
			final accept
		}
	}
	peer peer1 {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65000
				remote 65001
			}
			router-id 10.0.0.1
			family {
				ipv4/unicast {
					prefix {
						maximum 10000
					}
				}
			}
		}
		timer {
			receive-hold-time 90
			keepalive 30
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

option=env:var=ze.log.bgp.reactor.peer:value=debug

# The 180 s ceiling covers the fixed daemon (about 85 to 115 s) and the unfixed
# one (its cycle 3 wait is 15 to 20 s instead of 3.75 to 5 s), so a red run
# fails on the delay assertions below, not on the timeout. The runner stops ze
# as soon as ze-peer completes on connection 4.
option=timeout:value=180s
cmd=background:seq=1:exec=le test peer --port $PORT:stdin=peer
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp:timeout=180s

# Cycle 1 (AC-9 start): the first wait uses reconnectMin.
expect=stderr:pattern=(?s)msg="timing: safeRunOnce returned"[^\n]*attempt=1\b.*msg="timing: backoff starting"[^\n]*delay=5s\b.*msg="timing: connection attempt starting"[^\n]*attempt=2\b
# Cycle 2 (AC-9): a short Established session doubles the delay.
expect=stderr:pattern=(?s)msg="timing: safeRunOnce returned"[^\n]*attempt=2\b.*msg="timing: backoff starting"[^\n]*delay=10s\b.*msg="timing: connection attempt starting"[^\n]*attempt=3\b
# Cycle 3 precondition: the cycle lasted at least a minute, as the daemon
# measured it. A red here means the hold was short, not that the rule failed.
expect=stderr:pattern=msg="timing: safeRunOnce returned"[^\n]*attempt=3\b[^\n]*elapsed=[0-9]+m[0-9]
# Cycle 3 (AC-8): after the stable session the delay is back at reconnectMin.
expect=stderr:pattern=(?s)msg="timing: safeRunOnce returned"[^\n]*attempt=3\b.*msg="timing: backoff starting"[^\n]*delay=5s\b.*msg="timing: connection attempt starting"[^\n]*attempt=4\b
# The wrong outcome in the same window: the penalty kept and doubled again.
reject=stderr:pattern=(?s)msg="timing: safeRunOnce returned"[^\n]*attempt=3\b.*msg="timing: backoff starting"[^\n]*delay=20s\b.*msg="timing: connection attempt starting"[^\n]*attempt=4\b
# No cycle ended in ErrTeardown, which resets the delay by itself.
reject=stderr:pattern=msg="timing: safeRunOnce returned"[^\n]*session teardown
